 "answer": {
  "answer": "The study did not find 5 challenging cases. It confirmed one: scene 0e899dd3 with the lead pickup shifted 1 s earlier, slowed to 0.5x, and 150 ms of planner delay added (S18). Every run there ended with the ego hitting the back of the pickup, and the range puts the true failure rate above 0.5. This case is weaker than it looks. The triage of its videos and logs found the gap had already shrunk from about 15 m to about 6 m during the log replay, before the policy took control at 4.7 s. The policy then had about 0.5 s and slowed somewhat before the hit, so the collisions are mostly a result of the replay setup and the policy is not clearly at fault. On the other seven scenes, no setting failed in more than an occasional run, and most were run only once or twice, which gives a hint but not a rate.",
  "findings": [
   {
    "claim": "The only confirmed challenging case: on scene 0e899dd3, a time shift of -1 s, a 0.5x speed scale and a 150 ms planner delay made the ego rear-end the lead pickup in every kept run. Triage marks the policy as not at fault or unclear in every one of those runs, because the replay before handoff left only about 0.5 s and about 6 m to react. So this case says more about the handoff setup than about the policy's braking.",
    "settings": [
     "S18"
    ]
   },
   {
    "claim": "The same retiming on 0e899dd3 at 250 ms delay also failed in the same way (front contact with the pickup shortly after handoff, ego still on the recorded path). It was a single run, and 250 ms is above the brief's 200 ms cap.",
    "settings": [
     "S19"
    ]
   },
   {
    "claim": "On 0e899dd3, the failures depend on timing and speed. Changing the -1 s / 0.5x combination, whether to a smaller shift (-0.5 s, 0 s), a larger shift (-2 s, +1.5 s, +2 s) or a higher speed scale (1.25x to 2x), gave no failure. Most of these settings were run only once.",
    "settings": [
     "S16",
     "S17",
     "S20",
     "S21",
     "S22",
     "S23",
     "S24",
     "S25",
     "S26"
